/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A system at temperature T holds internal energy U.
   - The subtraction TS removes energy that cannot become work in a reversible isothermal step.
   - For a closed system with only pressure-volume work, dU = T dS − P dV.
   - A is Helmholtz free energy (J); S entropy (J/K); T absolute temperature (K); P pressure (Pa); V volume (m³).
   - G is Gibbs free energy (J); the other symbols are defined above.
   - At constant T and V, the second law gives dA ≤ 0 for a spontaneous change in a closed system with only PV work; dA = 0 at equilibrium.
   - the Gibbs–Helmholtz equation; G is Gibbs energy (J), H enthalpy (J), T absolute temperature (K).
   - Derivation: by the quotient rule, [∂(G/T)/∂T]P = (1/T)(∂G/∂T)P − G/T².
   - For an isothermal process on an ideal gas, ΔU = 0 and ΔH = 0.
   - Compression has P2 > P1, so ln(P2/P1) > 0 and both ΔA and ΔG are positive.
*/
