/* book exam traps: Writing G = U − TS instead of G = H − TS. Check that G = U + PV − TS; the PV term is missing in the wrong form. | Using Celsius in T. Every free-energy formula uses absolute temperature in kelvin. | Losing the sign for compression. If P2 > P1, ln(P2/P1) is positive, so ΔA and ΔG are positive; expansion gives negative values. | Swapping the equilibrium tests. dA ≤ 0 belongs to constant T,V; dG ≤ 0 belongs to constant T,P. | Forgetting that ΔA = ΔG is special to an ideal gas at constant T. For a real gas or a phase change, at constant T, ΔA = ΔU − TΔS and ΔG = ΔH − TΔS are different numbers; for boiling water at 1 atm, ΔG = 0 but ΔA = −3.05 kJ/mol. | Using ΔA = ΔU − TΔS when the temperature changes. The general form is ΔA = ΔU − Δ(TS) = ΔU − (T₂S₂ − T₁S₁). */
