/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a closed, simple compressible system: no chemical reaction, no shaft work, negligible kinetic and potential energy.
   - Define the other potentials by Legendre transforms: H = U + PV, A = U − TS, and G = H − TS = U + PV − TS.
   - U is internal energy (J), H enthalpy (J), A Helmholtz free energy (J), G Gibbs free energy (J).
   - Each left side is an exact differential; each right side contains only the natural variables of that potential.
   - An exact differential dz = M dx + N dy has M = (∂z/∂x)y and N = (∂z/∂y)x.
   - The subscript on each derivative says what is held constant.
   - Equality of mixed second derivatives of these potentials gives the Maxwell relations (see Maxwell relations).
   - Because U, H, A and G are state functions, integrate along any convenient reversible path between the same two states.
   - For an ideal gas, U and H depend only on T.
   - At constant temperature, a useful identity for real gases is (∂U/∂V)T = T(∂P/∂T)V − P.
*/
