/* book exam traps: Mixing up natural variables: U is natural in (S,V), not (T,V). H is natural in (S,P), not (T,P). | Using the wrong sign for P dV: dU = T dS − P dV and dA = −S dT − P dV both carry a minus sign on P dV. | Treating a partial derivative as a simple fraction: (∂U/∂V)S cannot be cancelled with dV. | Using dG = −S dT + V dP for a mixture whose composition changes. The full relation then needs chemical-potential terms, which belong to solution thermodynamics. | Confusing (∂U/∂V)T with (∂H/∂P)T; they are different derivatives and different identities. */
